# AETHER — Phases 4–7 Master Implementation Plan

This is the continuation of your existing document set (`01–04` = product spec and initial build prompts). It picks up exactly where `Prompts/04 Prompt (AntiGravity)` left off. It was written after a full read of every PRD, every prompt, and every file in your current codebase — not from assumptions.

## Why four more files instead of one

Your own build history (v0 → AntiGravity Phase 2 → Phase 2 patch → Phase 3) proves that narrow, bounded prompts fed one at a time to a coding agent produce clean results, and one giant prompt covering everything invites regressions and half-finished work. This plan keeps that discipline. Do not paste all four phase files into one agent conversation. Feed them **in order, one at a time**, and only start the next phase once the current one's acceptance checklist passes.

| File | Phase | What it does | Depends on |
|---|---|---|---|
| `05_Phase4_Bug_Bash_and_Auth_Guards.md` | 4 | Fixes everything already built: dead links, unwired buttons, missing 404, no real sign-out gate, three different hardcoded pricing numbers. Adds zero new pages. | Nothing — start here. |
| `06_Phase5_Missing_Pages_and_Onboarding.md` | 5 | Builds every page your own sitemap and mock data already call for but that doesn't exist yet: library, presets, compare, marketplace detail + publish, creator dashboard, billing subpages, account subpages, help, search, onboarding, blog. | Phase 4 (uses the fixed routes/links). |
| `07_Phase6_Node_System_Overhaul.md` | 6 | The "1000% more configurable" work: a real config-field schema, model/provider selection, editable prompts, a Deep Customization modal, applied first to Intelligence Agents then rolled out everywhere. | Phase 4. Independent of Phase 5 — can run in parallel if you use two agent sessions. |
| `08_Phase7_Graph_and_Config_Storage_Model.md` | 7 | Reshapes how bots/nodes/configs are stored — still `localStorage`, but normalized so it maps cleanly onto real relational tables later, and versioning/config history actually work. | Phase 6 (needs the new config shape to normalize). |

Total order if run serially: **4 → 5 → 6 → 7**. If you have two agent sessions, 5 and 6 can run concurrently after 4, and 7 waits for 6.

## What this plan is not

It is not a backend build. Every phase here stays inside the current architecture: Next.js 16 App Router, all client components, Zustand + `localStorage` persistence, everything mocked. No API routes, no database, no real Razorpay, no real model calls. That is deliberately the *next* plan, after this one — do not let an agent "helpfully" start wiring a real backend mid-phase. If an agent proposes that, stop it.

## Ground rules that apply to every phase below

Paste this section (or reference this file) at the top of whichever phase prompt you feed the agent, since it is not repeated in full in each phase file.

**Stack, as it exists today — do not change these:**
- Next.js 16 (App Router, `'use client'` on every interactive component), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind v4 (CSS-variable tokens in `app/globals.css`, no `tailwind.config`).
- State: Zustand. `lib/store.ts` (session/auth/theme/toasts/dev-switcher), `lib/workspace-store.ts` (bots/runs/presets/notifications, persisted), `lib/builder-store.ts` (canvas-local undo/redo state, not persisted directly — it's loaded from and saved back to the workspace store).
- Canvas: `@xyflow/react` (React Flow). Don't introduce a second canvas library or hand-roll positioning logic.
- UI kit: `@base-ui/react` primitives wrapped in `components/ui/*` (`Dialog`, `Menu`, `Select`, `Slider`, `Switch`, `Tabs`/`Segmented`). **Always reuse these** — never import a raw `@base-ui/react` primitive directly in a page, and never hand-roll a modal/dropdown with raw `useState` + conditional divs when `Dialog`/`Menu` already exist.
- Icons: `lucide-react`. Motion: `motion/react` for component-level transitions, `gsap` + `ScrollTrigger` (via `lib/use-reveal.ts`) for marketing-page scroll reveals only — don't mix them.
- Formatting: always `formatINR`, `formatPct`, `formatNumber`, `formatCompact`, `formatDate`, `relativeTime` from `lib/utils.ts`. Never hand-format a rupee value, a date, or a percentage inline. If a new format is needed (e.g. a duration), add one function to `lib/utils.ts`, don't inline it.
- IDs: `slugId(prefix)` from `lib/utils.ts` for anything new needing a client-generated id.

**Design language — "Aether Glass":**
- Frosted glass surfaces (`glass`, `glass-chrome` utility classes in `globals.css`), Apple-style easing (`EASE_AETHER` / `EASE_CSS` from `lib/utils.ts`), semantic colors `text-profit` / `text-loss` / `text-warn` / `text-gold` / `text-brand`, never raw green/red hex.
- Every list/table/detail page needs all three states already established elsewhere in the app: a loading `Skeleton`, an `EmptyState` (icon + title + one real explanation + one primary action — never a bare "No data"), and where relevant an `ErrorState` with retry. Copy the exact pattern from `app/app/bots/[botId]/page.tsx`'s `BotDetailSkeleton` + `EmptyState` combo.
- Every destructive action needs confirmation. The current codebase uses the browser's native `confirm()` in a couple of places (`bots/page.tsx`, `settings-tab.tsx`) — **replace these** with a proper `Dialog`-based `ConfirmDialog` component (build this once in Phase 4, reuse everywhere after). Native `confirm()` cannot be styled and looks broken next to a polished glass UI.
- Every non-trivial action produces a `toast` (`success` / `error` / `info` / `unlock` from `lib/store.ts`) — this pattern is already consistent almost everywhere; keep it that way for every new button you wire up.

**What "coming soon" is still allowed to mean after these four phases:** only things that require a real backend or real third-party integration — actual Razorpay checkout, real broker/live-money execution, real email delivery, a real LLM call. Everything else — every page, every button, every piece of data shown — must be real within the mocked/local-storage world: it must actually change state, actually persist across a refresh, and actually navigate somewhere that exists. "Coming soon" is not an acceptable outcome for a button that could instead just work against mock data.

**Never do this in any phase:**
- Don't invent a new visual language, spacing scale, or component pattern when an existing one in `components/ui/` already covers the need.
- Don't add `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` calls directly in a component — everything persisted goes through a Zustand `persist` store, same as today.
- Don't scope-creep. If you notice something unrelated to the current phase that's broken, note it in a comment or a running "found but not fixed" list at the end of the session — don't fix it in the middle of an unrelated phase. (Phase 4 is explicitly the phase where you *do* fix these, so most of what you'll notice is already listed there.)
- Don't touch `Iteration 101/` content or try to implement the "Quantum Edge" backend blueprint. That was an early idea, not a target for these phases.

